        "heading": "1 INTRODUCTION",
        "text": "Acclaimed as the third generation of artificial neural network (Maass, 1997), Spiking Neural Networks (SNNs) have brought brand-new inspiration in computational neuroscience. As the corresponding neuron fires spikes only when the current membrane potential exceeds the firing threshold, SNNs have the distinctive characteristics of binary output, high sparsity, and biological plausibility. Therefore, compared with traditional ANN models, SNNs can further improve computational efficiency and reduce power consumption, which facilitates its remarkable superiority in the application of neuromorphic chips (Merolla et al., 2014; Davies et al., 2018; DeBole et al., 2019). Considering that SNNs have not found an effective learning algorithm yet, ANN-SNN conversion and back-propagation through time (BPTT) are still the two most concerning training methods at present. Compared with BPTT, ANN-SNN conversion avoids the non-differentiable problem in the direct training procedure of SNNs and thus reduces the overall training complexity.\nANN-SNN conversion aims to establish the mapping relationship between the activation output and average firing rate. Traditional conversion methods exploit larger time-steps to overcome the conversion error and thus achieve high performance (Diehl et al., 2015). Many of the following works have attempted to optimize the performance from multiple perspectives, including using the soft-reset mechanism (Han et al., 2020; Rueckauer et al., 2017), proposing more adaptive activation functions (Cao et al., 2015; Ho & Chang, 2021; Bu et al., 2022b), adopting a trainable threshold (Sengupta et al., 2019; Ding et al., 2021; Bu et al., 2022a), etc. But these strategies cannot effectively eliminate the error caused by the deviation between the actual and desired firing rate, especially when the number of time-step is small. Some recent works consider to compensate the errors by introducing burst spikes (Li & Zeng, 2022) and signed spiking neurons (Li et al., 2022). Unlike these works, our paper attempts to eliminate the error with vanilla spiking neurons and answer the question of where the extreme performance of ANN-SNN conversion is.\nIn this paper, we observe and identify the source of the conversion error and propose an iterative optimization method based on shifting initial membrane potential, which can fulfill accurate mapping between ANNs and SNNs under ideal situation. Our main contributions are summarized as follows:\n1 We introduce the concept of offset spike to infer the deviation between the actual SNN firing rate and desired SNN firing rate. We point out that the case of firing one more (or less) spike is the main reason for conversion error.\n2 We propose a method to judge the offset spike based on the residual membrane potential and an optimization method to eliminate the conversion error through shifting up or down the initial membrane potential. We derive the optimal shifting distance and prove that one spike can be increased or decreased under this condition.\n3 We evaluate our methods on CIFAR-10/100 and ImageNet datasets. The proposed method outperforms the state-of-the-art ANN-SNN conversion methods using fewer time-steps. For example, we achieve 67.12% top-1 accuracy on ImageNet with only 6 time-steps (4 time-steps calibration and 2 time-steps inference). Moreover, it is worth noting that we have reached the same level of performance as BPTT under the condition of reducing memory and computing resources significantly.\n4 We discover that our proposed method has an iterative property. Under an ideal situation, the deviation within the range of k spikes will be eliminated entirely after adopting our approach for k times. After 4 iterations, the mean-square error between actual and desired firing rate of the output layer can reach 0.001 for the ResNet-20 model on CIFAR-100."
